#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921" :question/problem "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6faec47-b0e1-53cc-a122-ba924137a87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,8,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309472} {:answer/id #uuid "239ff84d-7ca6-561a-8043-85e62e7c8a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309473} {:answer/id #uuid "79bb4a2d-dafd-5be4-af5a-4aa992b2f677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309474} {:answer/id #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} {:answer/id #uuid "f344b47d-af36-5ae8-8c3d-e30f4f54e1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} :answer-field/id #uuid "0daf3085-8d89-5e14-92e1-e117adc2c4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f626f64-b605-58e9-8e55-f56146c3feb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129170" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8f7a45-4d82-58b9-bd8a-4a39eb05db24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨6,6,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331878} {:answer/id #uuid "c7782099-a0fd-53c6-86da-c019401e1704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=12,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331879} {:answer/id #uuid "49ef3c52-2524-518f-b2f3-1a0a54daaf75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331880} {:answer/id #uuid "958eab3e-848e-5daa-8090-2f55167ef79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨4,6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331881} {:answer/id #uuid "b33ba095-c0ae-5126-8eb9-0d3719014ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b33ba095-c0ae-5126-8eb9-0d3719014ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331882} :answer-field/id #uuid "639a9cca-ac75-56eb-870d-145fc313d159" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "583166f3-f984-5b08-b009-d0467651a92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129215" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-3z=0$ and $2y-3z-6=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,3,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,3,0⟩+t⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=⟨6,6,4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b41fa7c-383b-551d-8e7d-195af093e233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309486} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef7e40-b70b-5a85-8a24-375e22f4f302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309487} {:answer/id #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} {:answer/id #uuid "2f47da4a-b316-542d-9add-3c553c97e434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309489} {:answer/id #uuid "eca8e996-c8fa-5d8f-b034-7e46ddd5ba4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} :answer-field/id #uuid "8149ef53-3f86-5b48-a7e1-8fa0afbc8615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "923148c0-0ecb-5f91-a025-b18771e50157" :question/math-academy-id "q-129224" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b9f991-4144-574d-b26f-5126b1b33b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331885} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ec36b8-e877-5158-9f63-24320f697d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331886} {:answer/id #uuid "a5918a05-fcd8-5042-af53-53d9846fbcdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331887} {:answer/id #uuid "d66d9ffa-275f-5859-b1ba-449c843e4255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331888} {:answer/id #uuid "5d743de0-62e6-5fc0-80b4-ce5ef89010b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d9ffa-275f-5859-b1ba-449c843e4255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331888} :answer-field/id #uuid "b141f848-4c27-5b0c-bf2a-351e8d6af56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "959d7c1a-33e8-592b-b5a5-d7f6a288fc45" :question/math-academy-id "q-129241" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c6d4629-4b4d-58b2-84f4-efc7a0475cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348755} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a591bc-546a-53a8-95a1-149c1c96f456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348756} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1d458d-ad7f-5879-9492-422252cdc8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348757} {:answer/id #uuid "49af3ebc-841a-58b3-89ed-84c600117d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348758} {:answer/id #uuid "14194db7-0721-550f-a526-89686b6a0c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49af3ebc-841a-58b3-89ed-84c600117d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348758} :answer-field/id #uuid "aecc0f8f-d563-5c1d-b722-ee5aeb098b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32658552-b15a-5af7-93c5-13bdae918ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129258" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bc9956-3f2a-5751-9955-380cc068e4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb4e7da-d423-537d-b6d1-eca94ff0ffac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309501} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd6c990-9990-5e92-9979-5092fd03374b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309502} {:answer/id #uuid "6a28c4a1-3a4b-5b47-8e2a-096224035b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309503} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbba08a-aee5-5bc2-b685-3b92ad7ff18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbba08a-aee5-5bc2-b685-3b92ad7ff18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6f1dbf-64e4-5fbc-ab2f-a0d761ede179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0531b19e-756e-5c97-abd0-1f5e648bd0cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129259" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bac37965-8c39-5fe8-8477-dca2f06947a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317934} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baef66-cb25-55f8-9dcb-524cc89acc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317935} {:answer/id #uuid "4967c162-3d17-5ddc-89c4-29461e0fb076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317936} {:answer/id #uuid "434a6c0a-9995-57aa-ae40-2f5688e38d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317937} {:answer/id #uuid "d0910555-34fd-5736-bb97-1bfe04b7cec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0910555-34fd-5736-bb97-1bfe04b7cec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317938} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ddbfadd-a8b1-5f1f-ad22-809f6ba2beaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22e39499-5747-5698-af95-b4fc0c4d8191" :question/math-academy-id "q-129261" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca85272d-c8e6-5de8-ae5f-276509834959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309535} {:answer/id #uuid "a56f4364-17c3-5089-86db-c35c7232a8ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309536} {:answer/id #uuid "60692775-03e2-554e-a6c2-463e42103a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309537} {:answer/id #uuid "2a60b638-d8b1-57b4-b880-3bd292690ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb7bccb-6b13-538d-a7d9-6876fef93242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a60b638-d8b1-57b4-b880-3bd292690ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d55ebd-d47b-5681-8adf-7c27083248c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b23b0cec-a767-5778-8e67-5c10a6b25080" :question/math-academy-id "q-71347" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-y+6=0$ and $y+2z-2=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x-0+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2z-2=0\\,⟹\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+2z-2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i-4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3-1=-4,$ and $b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd8ed127-be85-544a-bc03-1806ffa03548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317948} {:answer/id #uuid "ada6b8f6-5526-5e24-ad84-0b05b1181052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317949} {:answer/id #uuid "bce644e9-3d29-5fa1-af4d-33771a072e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950} {:answer/id #uuid "e881c0c4-9a27-5d88-9335-7ba7bebb29d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317951} {:answer/id #uuid "2e11eee1-0155-5d84-96b6-03d1ea939615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd8ed127-be85-544a-bc03-1806ffa03548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317948} :answer-field/id #uuid "dddf3ab9-17c6-51d5-a9ad-06d3404c0068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61cf4d39-9683-533e-9394-f4988b6cbe81" :question/math-academy-id "q-71446" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$"}]}
